WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:12.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the conclusion to the lords of shadow  saga exhibits peaks of brilliance and  art direction and sound design and its  new free camera system is a definite  improvement over the first games tightly  choreographed camera however to fully  enjoy the best parts within you must  endure a handful of drab settings and  boring stealth puzzles along the way at  times is enough to make you want to put  the controller down especially after the  story's puttering take off but stick  around until the end and you'll enjoy  eye catching boss fights and a  satisfying conclusion to the narrative  that ultimately reduces the negative  impact of the games earlier issues the  Prince of Darkness Dracula is in a tough  position at the start of lords of Shadow  two centuries into his tenure as the  lord of vampires he suffers under the  weight of immortality and the memories  of his once beloved family that is until  he awakes in a Cathedral smack dab in  the middle of a modern metropolis  shortly thereafter he's confronted by  his arch nemesis  Sobek who betrayed Dracula centuries  earlier though Beck's quest for world  domination has hidden obstacles  Satan is on the rise this all comes  after a rousing prologue set in the past  which sees Dracula full-strength  battling righteous warriors in and  around his unholy castle for all the  excitement offered there the true start  to lords of Shadow two's plot with xot  back is comparatively deflating your  motivation to hunt someone else's  enemies doesn't inspire excitement or  interest plus you're immediately thrust  into one of the most bland and  uninspiring settings to be found an  industrial scientific complex replete  with sheetmetal red pipes and security  guards as the game carries on however  Dracula finds himself back in time and  the gameplay returns to its roots combat  and tormented environments take center  stage and with the game's new free  moving camera and an emphasis on  exploration both aspects feel fresh and  new  the first lords of Shadow 2 Castlevania  into the realm of God of War style  combat with an emphasis on combining  direct and wide attacks but a rewarding  parrying system it's largely the same  this time around - but there are a few  new tools to play around with along with  a rewarding skill mastery system and  lords of Shadow - you learn skills for  each of your three weapons independently  skills are learned by spending  experience points granted during combat  when each skill has a gauge that fills  with use the fragmentation of the move  lists delays your efficacy in battles  somewhat but it also allows you to focus  on customization more than before each  weapon features a wide swath of skills  to learn opening the game up to  different types of combat strategies  when you aren't smashing demon skulls  you spend quite a lot of time exploring  your environment by default your  objective is often highlighted on your  map hints like swarms of bats tip you  off to handholds for climbing and ledges  for leaping however unlike the first  game there are many alternate paths to  explore in search of treasure while not  entirely game-changing the openness  feels appropriate given the wide world  around you apart from some occasionally  frustrating path finding only the map  stands in your way  it's only ever represented on a piece by  piece basis then the larger world map is  just an illustration with names and  numbers attached it doesn't hurt the  moment-to-moment poking around but it  doesn't entice you to backtrack either  and besides the deeper you get into  lords of Shadow 2 the better it gets  halfway through the game the narrative  and frequency of impressive set pieces  begins to steamroll ahead and the last  thing you want to do is look back zou  Beck takes a side line to Dracula's  ambitions and you and he begin to  understand why he's really going to such  great lengths to thwart Satan can that  point on you spend almost every minute  around and within sublime examples of  Gothic architecture  it's a pleasing change of pace after  slogging through boring environments  waiting for something to grab your  attention the contrast between the two  halves of the game is really hard to  ignore and even though you have to force  your way through mediocrity to get to  the good stuff the conclusion and a few  hours leading up to it justify the time  spent steeped in boredom in frustration  realizing this it's clear that lords of  Shadow 2 could have been a much shorter  game still though its stealth sections  and drab modern settings represent the  worst elements of the three-part saga  the tail end of the game contains the  very best of every aspect even better  the final act wraps up the lords of  shadow trilogy with authority and the  game's final breaths leave you both  gasping for air and sighing in relief  it may not strike newcomers to Gabriel's  tail with such force but it's  nonetheless a surprising and fulfilling  conclusion to the singular chapter of  lords of Shadow 2 if you have the  patience to get through the rough start  you'll discover a much better game  waiting for you on the other side  [Music]

